A||||12||Steven||Coolent Temp Sensor||||5||
Z||000000||Steven||06-22-2001||11:56 AM||||Has any one ever had a coolent temp sensor go bad, but not throw any kind of trouble codes? What did you car act like if you had that happen?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Steve Normington||06-22-2001||12:39 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Steven:<BR><B>Has any one ever had a coolent temp sensor go bad, but not throw any kind of trouble codes? What did you car act like if you had that happen?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Are you talking the temp sensor for the dash gage, the sensor for the ECM, or the sensor for the cold start valve? I assume you don't mean the fan switch. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Steven||06-22-2001||01:36 PM||||I ment the coolent temp sensor for the ECM.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Steve Normington||06-22-2001||01:46 PM||||If the sensor goes bad, then most likely the ECM is going to think that the engine is cooler that it really is. You should see higher operating temps and more fuel consumption. If its hard to start when hot, that could also be a sign of a bad sensor. A bad connection to the sensor can also cause the same problem. The ECM won't store any trouble codes until the sensor reading gets beyond the accectible range.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Steven||06-22-2001||02:10 PM||||Well the thing that it does is when I start it cold, it won't find an idle. It jumps up and down looking for an idle. So I know that the iac is working, because it is going thur it whole range of motion. Once the engine warms up to operating temp, it is fine. Which means in closed loop (I think that is when the ecm get it info from the o2 sensor) it runs fine. Idles perfect. I thought maybe the cold start injector might be leaking, but it that was the case, I would run rich all the time, nut just cold starts. So that is when I started thinking that maybe the ecm coolent temp sensor might be on its last leg. How is my logic so far? <BR>Also before anyone asks, no, no codes have been stored in the ecm, and no the check engine light has not come on.<p>[This message has been edited by Steven (edited 06-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Steven||06-22-2001||02:12 PM||||Woops, double posted. Sorry.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Steven (edited 06-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula 18||06-22-2001||02:15 PM||||The coolant temp sensor for the gauge is totally seprate, right? <P>weird Idls can be cause by a vaccume leak or bad EGR or sometimes even an alternator starting to go bad. Check the ERG valve and tube, after that look for leaks. <P>Brian <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Steven||06-22-2001||02:20 PM||||Going by the GM service manual, the way to test for vac. leak is to ground the ALDL conn. and turn the ign. on. wait 30 sec for the iac to seat it self. Unplug iac. Start engine. If the engine runs 800 rpm or higher, you have a vac. leak. My car runs at 500 (-+50). The alternator is brand new. And yes the gauge sensor is different than the ECM sensor. The gauge sensor is the on in the head to the left of the #5 cylinder. The ECM sensor is the one below the thermostat housing. Two wires, one yellow, one black.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Steven||06-22-2001||06:10 PM||||Does anyone have any more ideas? &lt;bump&gt;||||reg||3
Z||000009||ArchMagi||09-13-2001||01:25 AM||||Steven,<P>will that vac leak trick work for the 4cyl's as well?||||reg||1
Z||000010||merlin||09-13-2001||08:41 AM||||Steven,<BR>I had a similar problem, where the idle would fluctuate until the car reached operating temperature and it DID  turn out to be the coolant sensor.  Like your car, my car was not throwing any codes because the resistance from the sensor was in the correct range even though it was an incorrect reading.  The coolant sensor is a thermostatic resistor, which means the resistance in the sensor changes as the temperature changes.  In my case the sensor was telling the ECM that the engine was warm when it was in fact cold.  I was able to verify this by testing the resistance coming from the coolant sensor with a multimeter.  However, you probably don't have a multimeter so I won't go into the test procedures and it is also pretty difficult to get the test leads into such a small place.  The coolant sensor is around $20 and not that difficult to replace.  Just make sure you use some Teflon tape on the threads of the sensor so it does not leak.<P>Good Luck  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||ArchMagi||09-14-2001||07:24 PM||||I quote theorge<P>"Don't use Teflon thread sealing tape on grounded shell NPT sensors. It will likely interfere with getting a good electrical connection through the threads."<P>one says yay, one says nay.<P>------------------<BR>87 Coupe with 168,000KM<BR>4cyl, 5 speed||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||09-14-2001||07:31 PM||||Like I said in the Sensor QRG..... grounded shell...<P>ECM's coolant and MAT sensors are electrically isolated from the shell. It is perfectly fine to use teflon tape on it. <P>The ones for the gauge and fan... those are grounded shells and you shouldn't use teflon tape on them. those get pipe dope. Some people use a tiny bit of RTV silicone on them....<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-14-2001).]||||reg||1
